<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adam of Bremen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1085</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adam of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> was a German chronicler who wrote the Deeds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, a history of the archbishopric of Hamburg-Bremen, around 1075. Its fourth book is the most important early description of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and the Baltic, including the pagan temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> and peoples such as the Sambians. He drew on information from the Danish king Sweyn Estridsson.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen.jpg" alt="Adam of Bremen" width="300" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam of Bremen († 1081/1085) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Utdrag_ur_Gesta_Hammaburgensis_ecclesiae_pontificum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
